    "minutes": 4,
    "sections": [
      [
        "Calculate the consumable difference before the capital saving",
        "The earlier Tech Pump recommendation asked a useful question: what does the tubing cost? It stopped short of the evidence needed to answer it. Without an OEM, model, approved-set list and calibration instructions, a claim to accept ordinary IV tubing is not a purchase specification. The approved set determines whether the manufacturer's performance claims apply. Name the device before treating its apparent flexibility as an advantage. For compactplus and Agilia, build the same annual basket of the required approved set variants. Multiply expected use by delivered prices, then add minimum-order, freight and expiry costs. That exposes the recurring difference before a discounted pump price dominates the discussion. Replenishment matters just as much: a cheap set that arrives irregularly can leave a complete pump unavailable. Standard Infusomat compactplus uses specified sets. A seller referring to a different tubing-compatible compactplus variant must identify that exact variant and its approved list. The same discipline applies to a donated Alaris or Spectrum family name: it does not identify an approved administration set. When the consumables basket is sustainable, compare the delivered pump, clamp, battery and service package on equal terms."
      ],
      [
        "Standardize the syringe supply before the front panels",
        "Perfusor compactplus and Agilia SP MC deserve a separate syringe-pump decision. First match the required functions and sizes to syringes the service can actually buy. Physical fit does not establish validated delivery, and the smallest displayed rate increment does not establish equivalent performance for every intended use. Perfusor's shared compactplus interface can simplify an existing Infusomat training and service arrangement. Agilia SP MC can be the stronger ownership choice where its approved syringe supply and regional technical support are already dependable. Keeping different volumetric and syringe families may be sensible if it preserves those advantages. Buy a matching family when it reduces real work; do not discard a supported system to make the pumps look uniform."
      ],
      [
        "A smart-pump feature needs an owner",
        "Available drug libraries, WiFi and profiles are capabilities, not an installed hospital program. The useful purchasing distinction is whether the service has people who will approve the configuration, maintain it and support any server or interface. Fund those tasks alongside the feature. If the integration will not be operated, judge the required standalone configuration rather than assigning value to an unused network. A donated Alaris fleet makes that distinction particularly tangible. Inventory controllers, modules, software versions, batteries, approved sets and service history as one system. Existing staff familiarity and technical capacity can justify keeping a complete fleet. Mixed hardware that cannot be configured and verified adds work before it adds usable channels. The relevant donation cost is the amount needed to bring each required channel into service."
      ],
      [
        "Two correction records to check on donated fleets",
        "Baxter's 2025 Spectrum/Spectrum IQ action concerns identified units that may lack motor-mounting screws, including an issue associated with servicing. Check the serial and documented inspection status; this is not evidence that every Spectrum pump has the defect. It strengthens the case for traceable refurbishment rather than a power-on-only seller description. The 2025 BD correction reviewed here concerns particular Alaris Systems Manager and Care Coordination Engine Adapter software versions under automated-programming conditions. It must not be presented as a recall of every standalone Alaris pump. A networked offer needs the relevant software and correction record in addition to hardware checks. For a new compactplus-versus-Agilia purchase, the strongest available evidence concerns approved consumables, documented features and an identifiable service route. It does not establish comparative failure rates. For a used pump, condition and completed verification can change the decision more than its family name. No unit was tested in this research."
      ]
    ],
