    "sections": [
      [
        "Buy enough usable scopes to run the planned list",
        "Picture an ordinary working day with one scope unavailable. Which examinations can still be completed while it is being processed or repaired? That answer is more useful than the processor's feature list. A service with a single suitable scope can gain more capacity from another accepted compatible scope than from a tower upgrade that leaves the same bottleneck. Build the comparison around exact gastroscope and colonoscope models, their condition, and the number required to maintain the planned service. Include a monitor, image capture, water-bottle and air/water accessories, leak testing and a workable spare strategy. Match channel size and reach to the clinician-approved examination list. Price common scope repairs separately: a low processor price says little about the future cost of a damaged channel or insertion tube."
      ],
      [
        "Stay with Olympus when it preserves useful equipment",
        "CV-190/CLV-190 has a practical advantage for an existing Olympus service if compatible scopes, accessories and staff familiarity can be retained. It does not justify buying a poor-condition scope fleet. Keep a functioning CV-160 when its individual components still support the work; replace or expand it when a complete newer package offers a more sustainable repair and supply arrangement. The purchase should follow the required capability and support, rather than a fixed ceiling on model generation. For a new service, give Fujifilm EP-6000 and PENTAX IMAGINA a fair comparison. Fujifilm integrates LED illumination and processing; PENTAX puts illumination in the compatible i10c scope's distal tip. Those designs change the equipment and repair basket. Ask where the expensive replacement component sits and who can supply it. Do not assume an old EPK-1000 scope will transfer to IMAGINA."
      ],
      [
        "Reprocessing determines whether the apparent capacity is real",
        "Complete the cleaning, processing, drying, storage and traceability plan before expanding the fleet. More scopes will not solve an unfunded processing bottleneck, and a donated reprocessor does not establish compatibility. STERIS prohibits processing devices excluded by their manufacturer or absent from its compatibility finder. An existing supported SYSTEM 1E installation can therefore favor particular compatible scopes; an unsupported or mismatched machine can add cost without adding usable capacity. The exact SYSTEM 1E model also changes handling requirements. For the P6700, its manual requires critical devices to be used immediately or processed again before use; semicritical devices may be handled and stored like high-level-disinfected endoscopes. Identify the offered model and use its instructions. Do not apply that configuration's workflow to every SYSTEM 1E generation or treat liquid chemical processing as permission to abbreviate cleaning."
      ],
      [
        "Give a repair promise a price and a timescale",
        "A written repair offer should identify the scopes it accepts, inspection or refurbishment scope, major exclusions and turnaround. Add outbound and return freight, and decide how the service runs during the wait. Local repair and a usable loan arrangement can outweigh a modest capital saving; an overseas workshop without continuity provision can leave the same room idle for longer. No complete comparable system prices or head-to-head field reliability results were verified here. That limits a universal brand ranking, but it does not prevent a decision: favor the package that keeps the required scope fleet available within the funded processing and repair plan. If those continuing costs cannot be sustained, phase the service or fund dependable referral before adding a tower."
      ]
    ],
